Copy files from Local Folders to GitHub

To copy files from your local system to GitHub, you can either upload them directly through the website or use Git commands in your terminal.
Before you begin, ensure you have created a repository on GitHub (click the "+" icon in the top-right corner, select New repository, give it a name, and leave it empty). 
Copy the Repository URL provided on the setup page.

Method 1: Using the Terminal / Git Commands (Recommended)
This is the standard and most reliable method for uploading full project structures or large sets of files.
1. Open your terminal (or Git Bash on Windows) and navigate to your local folder: Gitbash - cd /path/to/your/local/folder
2. Initialize Git in your local project folder: git init
3. Stage all your files to prepare them for the upload: git add .
4. Commit the files with a descriptive tracking message: git commit -m "Initial commit of local files"
5. Rename the default branch to match GitHub's default standard (main): git branch -M main
6. Link your local folder to your remote GitHub repository (replace REMOTE-URL with the URL you copied from GitHub): git remote add origin REMOTE-URL
7. Push your files up to GitHub: git push -u origin main

Note: You may be prompted to authenticate your GitHub account through your browser or via a Personal Access Token.
